{#
    The way on from this page: the pages either side of it, at the end of the
    column.

    Nothing of the renderer's is being replaced here — its own prev/next block
    has been commented out of the core template for years, and there is no
    `next` or `prev` to read. So the order comes from `Navigation\Pager`: the
    tree flattened, which is the order the rail lists and the order somebody
    reading a manual front to back would take.

    What that order becomes is `sds-nav-pager`'s, not this file's. Which end each
    control sits at, which glyph carries the direction and what a reader who
    cannot see it hears are decisions about a component, and a template that
    made them would be a second place they could be made differently.

    Handed over as four attributes and not as one object: a label and a target
    each fit in a string, so nothing of this renderer's data model reaches the
    component, and a caller working from some other tree fills the same four.
#}
{%- set steps = pager() -%}
{%- if steps.previous or steps.next %}
<sds-nav-pager
    {%- if steps.previous %} previous-href="{{ steps.previous.href }}" previous-label="{{ steps.previous.label }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if steps.next %} next-href="{{ steps.next.href }}" next-label="{{ steps.next.label }}"{% endif -%}
></sds-nav-pager>
{%- endif -%}
